import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={heatTransfer:{theory:{concept:"[[Heat transfer is the passing of internal energy from one body to another without doing work; by itself, heat always flows from a hotter body to a colder one.]] It happens in three ways: by conduction (through a solid, particle to particle), by convection (carried by a moving liquid or gas) and by radiation (as invisible infrared light, even through empty space). A hot drink in a cup loses its heat in all three ways at once; which one wins depends on the cup, the air and the surface."},section:{flows:{title:"Three ways heat travels",text:"In the picture the hot liquid is in a cup. Its heat first crosses the wall of the cup by conduction. Then it leaves the outer surface in two ways at the same time: air that touches the surface warms up and drifts away (convection), and the surface sends out infrared light (radiation). The bars on the right show how much power each way carries; the conducted power always equals the sum of the other two, because the heat has nowhere else to go."},conduction:{title:"Conduction",text:"[[In a solid, heat passes from fast-vibrating particles to their slower neighbours.]] The rate is P = k\xB7A\xB7\u0394T/\u03B4: bigger for a larger area A, a bigger temperature difference \u0394T and a better conductor (a larger conductivity k), and smaller for a thicker wall \u03B4. Metals (steel k = 16 W/(m\xB7K)) conduct heat well, glass (1) and porcelain (1.5) poorly, and foam (0.033) is a very good insulator, because it is mostly trapped air. That is why a foam cup keeps its outside cool while a metal cup burns your hand."},convection:{title:"Convection",text:"[[A liquid or gas carries heat along with its own motion.]] Air next to a hot surface warms up, expands, becomes lighter and rises, and cooler air flows in to take its place \u2014 natural convection. A fan or the wind makes the air move faster and carries heat away much more quickly: forced convection. We write the loss as P = h\xB7A\xB7(T_s \u2212 T_air); the heat transfer coefficient h grows with the air speed (and weakly with the temperature difference). Blowing on hot soup works because of this."},radiation:{title:"Radiation",text:"[[Every body radiates infrared light, and the hotter it is, the more: P = \u03B5\xB7\u03C3\xB7A\xB7(T\u2074 \u2212 T_air\u2074) with the temperature in kelvin.]] Doubling the (kelvin) temperature makes sixteen times the power. The emissivity \u03B5 tells how well the surface radiates: a dull, black surface (\u03B5 near 1) radiates well, a shiny metal one (\u03B5 near 0.05) hardly at all. Radiation needs no matter: it is how the Sun's heat crosses empty space. At the temperature of a cup of tea it carries about as much heat as convection."},series:{title:"The wall and the surface in series",text:"The heat has to pass through the wall and then through the surface layer: two steps one after the other, like resistances in series. Each has a thermal resistance (for the wall R = \u03B4/(k\xB7A)), and the temperature drops across each step in proportion to its resistance. A thin metal wall has a tiny resistance, so almost all of the drop is outside, in the air, and the surface is nearly as hot as the liquid. A thick foam wall has a large resistance, so almost all the drop is inside the wall and the outside stays cool."},newton:{title:"How the liquid cools",text:"The liquid loses heat at the rate P, and m\xB7c\xB7dT/dt = \u2212P. For small differences P is proportional to T \u2212 T_air, which gives Newton's law of cooling: the difference falls exponentially, T \u2212 T_air = (T\u2080 \u2212 T_air)\xB7e^(\u2212t/\u03C4). The cooling is fastest at the start (the difference is greatest) and slows down more and more: the same time that takes the difference from 70\xB0 to 35\xB0 takes it from 35\xB0 to 17.5\xB0. This time is the half-life t\xBD = 0.69\xB7\u03C4 shown in the readings."},flask:{title:"The vacuum flask",text:'A thermos is made to stop all three ways at once. Its double wall has a vacuum between the layers, and there is no matter to conduct heat or to carry it by convection. Both surfaces facing the gap are silvered, so they radiate very little (\u03B5 near 0.05) and reflect the radiation back. A stopper blocks the air above the liquid. A good flask loses less than a watt, so tea stays hot for many hours. Try "Vacuum gap" with a shiny surface and a thick wall.'},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Why does a foam cup keep the tea warm longer than a thin metal cup? (A thick insulating wall: low k, large R.) What does a fan change? (Convection: h grows with the air speed.) Why is a shiny cup better for keeping heat? (Low emissivity: little radiation.) If the room is the same temperature as the tea, how big is the heat flow? (Zero.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{conduction:"Conduction through the wall: conductivity \xD7 area \xD7 temperature difference / thickness",convection:"Convection from the surface to the air (h: heat transfer coefficient)",radiation:"Radiation from the surface (temperatures in kelvin, \u03B5: emissivity)",constant:"The Stefan\u2013Boltzmann constant",heat:"Heat released by the liquid as it cools (m: mass, c: specific heat)",newton:"Newton's law of cooling: the temperature difference falls exponentially",resistance:"Thermal resistance of the wall; the temperature drop across it"},variable:{P:"Power: heat flowing per second",k:"Thermal conductivity, W/(m\xB7K)",h:"Heat transfer coefficient, W/(m\xB2\xB7K)",epsilon:"Emissivity of the surface (0\u20131)",T:"Temperature",delta:"Thickness of the wall"},assumption:{uniform:"The liquid is at one temperature everywhere (it is stirred), and only the liquid holds heat: the wall's own heat capacity is ignored.",evaporation:"There is no evaporation: in a real open cup evaporation takes away more heat than all three ways together.",flat:'The wall is treated as a flat layer and the surface has a fixed area of 0.03 m\xB2; the convection formula is an approximation, and the "vacuum gap" is a wall of very low effective conductivity (a real flask also loses heat through its stopper).',scale:"Not to scale: the dots show where and how strongly heat flows, not real particles or photons. The bars use one scale of 80 W. Time is sped up."},param:{material:"Wall of the cup",emissivity:"Outer surface (emissivity \u03B5)",airSpeed:"Air flowing past",thickness:"Thickness of the wall",mass:"Mass of the liquid",startTemp:"Starting temperature of the liquid",ambient:"Temperature of the room",timeScale:"Speed of time (real seconds per second)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{material:"A good conductor lets the heat out fast; foam and a vacuum gap almost stop it.",emissivity:"A dull black surface radiates a lot, a shiny metal one very little.",airSpeed:"A fan or the wind carries heat away from the surface.",thickness:"A thicker wall resists the heat more.",mass:"More liquid holds more heat, so it cools more slowly.",startTemp:"A cold drink (lower than the room) warms up instead.",ambient:"The air and the walls of the room.",timeScale:"How many seconds of real cooling pass in one second on screen."},material:{steel:"Steel (conducts well)",glass:"Glass",ceramic:"Porcelain",foam:"Foam (insulator)",vacuum:"Vacuum gap (flask)"},preset:{shiny:"Shiny metal",matte:"Dull metal",ceramic:"Glass or porcelain",black:"Black",still:"Still air",breeze:"Breeze",fan:"Fan",thin:"Thin",normal:"Normal",thick:"Thick"},measure:{temperature:"Temperature of the liquid",power:"Heat flowing out (through the wall)",minutes:"Time",surface:"Temperature of the outer surface",convection:"Power lost by convection",radiation:"Power lost by radiation",convShare:"Share of convection",radShare:"Share of radiation",halfLife:"Time for the difference from the room to halve",hConvection:"Convection coefficient h, W/(m\xB2\xB7K)",hRadiation:"Effective coefficient of radiation, W/(m\xB2\xB7K)",wallResistance:"Thermal resistance of the wall, K/W",surfaceResistance:"Thermal resistance of the surface, K/W",tau:"Time to cool at the present rate",heatLost:"Heat lost so far",conductivity:"Conductivity of the wall k, W/(m\xB7K)",ambient:"Temperature of the room"},view:{fan:"Fan, air {speed}",conductionBar:"Conduction: {power}",convectionBar:"Convection: {power}",radiationBar:"Radiation: {power}",sum:"Conduction = convection + radiation (bars to {scale})",conductionName:"Conduction through the wall",convectionName:"Convection: rising air",radiationName:"Radiation: infrared",surface:"outer surface {temperature}",air:"air {temperature}",cooling:"The liquid is hotter than the room: heat flows outwards",warming:"The liquid is colder than the room: heat flows inwards",time:"t = {time}",flowsOption:"Rising air and rays",arrowsOption:"Heat flowing through the wall"},graph:{cooling:"Temperature against time",losses:"Heat lost: total, convection and radiation"},challenge:{convectionWins:{title:"The wind wins",description:"Make convection carry at least 70 % of the heat away from the cup.",hint:"Turn the fan on: moving air makes convection much stronger."},shiny:{title:"A shiny cup",description:"Make radiation carry at most 10 % of the heat (while the cup is still losing heat).",hint:"Choose the shiny metal surface: low emissivity."},keepWarm:{title:"Keep it warm",description:"Make a cup in which the difference from the room halves only after 2 hours or more. Let the run finish.",hint:"Use the vacuum gap, a thick wall and a shiny surface \u2014 like a flask."},fast:{title:"Cool it fast",description:"Make the liquid lose half of its difference from the room within 8 minutes. Let the run finish.",hint:"A thin steel wall, a black surface, a fan, and little liquid (100 g)."},hotSurface:{title:"A hot outside",description:"With the liquid at 80 \xB0C or more, make the outer surface of the cup less than 1 degree cooler than the liquid.",hint:"A thin wall of steel hardly resists the heat."},holdable:{title:"A cup you can hold",description:"With the liquid at 80 \xB0C or more, keep the outer surface at 45 \xB0C or below.",hint:"A thick wall of foam: most of the temperature drop happens inside the wall."},flask:{title:"A flask",description:"With the liquid at 80 \xB0C or more, make the cup lose no more than 1 W.",hint:"A vacuum gap, a thick wall and a shiny surface. Check the total power."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
